<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagarjuna Sagar Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nagarjuna Sagar Dam is a large masonry dam on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> river on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built between 1955 and 1967 for irrigation and hydroelectric power. Its reservoir flooded the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a>, site of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a>. Before the flooding, archaeologists excavated the valley and moved several monuments to an island museum and to higher ground.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarjuna-sagar-dam.jpg" alt="Nagarjuna Sagar Dam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sumanthk at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NagarjunaSagarDam.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Survey work for a dam across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> began in 1903, when the Nizam had British engineers start it. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> inaugurated construction on 10 December 1955, and work went on for twelve years under the engineering leadership of Kanuri Lakshmana Rao, entirely with local know-how. Raja Vasireddy Ramagopala Krishna Maheswara Prasad, known as the Muktyala Raja, lobbied for the project and donated land for it. The dam was the tallest masonry dam in the world at the time and the first of the major projects termed modern temples that were begun to achieve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-revolution">Green Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The masonry dam is 124 metres tall from its deepest foundation and 1.6 kilometres long, with 26 flood gates, and is joined by an earth dam 3414 m long. Its reservoir has a gross storage capacity of 11.472 billion cubic metres. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> released water into the left and right bank canals on 4 August 1967. The right canal, the Jawahar canal, runs 203 km and the left, the Lalbahadur Shastri canal, 179 km, irrigating land in districts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. The hydroelectric plant, its first unit commissioned on 7 March 1978 and its eighth on 24 December 1985, has a capacity of 815.6 MW.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The reservoir submerged 54 villages, 48 in Nalgonda and 6 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a>, and affected 24,000 people, whose relocation was completed by 2007. It also flooded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a>, the Buddhist settlement that had been the capital of the Ikshvaku dynasty in the 1st and 2nd centuries, where excavations yielded 30 Buddhist monasteries as well as artwork and inscriptions. The diversion of the river caused erosion of fluorine-rich volcanic rocks that contaminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> in Nalgonda, shrank Kolleru Lake and brought salination to coastal land in Diviseema, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> ceased to be navigable. Between 1995 and 2004 a Krishna water project was built to carry water from the reservoir to Hyderabad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagarjuna Sagar Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vijayapuri was flooded by the <b>Nagarjuna Sagar</b> dam in the 1960s, after excavated monuments were moved to higher ground.</blockquote><a href="/state/ikshvaku-dynasty">Ikshvaku dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
